WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.284
Sovereign AI systems need observability because private operation does not remove operational uncertainty.

2
00:00:07.284 --> 00:00:19.372
Teams need to understand how systems are used, whether they are performing well, whether sensitive data is handled correctly, and whether outputs are reliable enough for the workflow.

3
00:00:19.372 --> 00:00:30.608
Useful signals may include request volume, latency, model errors, retrieval quality, tool calls, user feedback, safety events, cost signals, and operational failures.

4
00:00:30.608 --> 00:00:34.094
Prompt and response logging require careful design.

5
00:00:34.094 --> 00:00:47.267
Logs are useful for debugging, evaluation, abuse investigation, and support, but they may contain confidential business data, personal information, customer material, or other sensitive content.

6
00:00:47.267 --> 00:01:00.363
A responsible logging plan includes redaction, retention limits, access controls, sampling, privacy review, and a clear distinction between operational telemetry and sensitive content capture.

7
00:01:00.363 --> 00:01:03.617
Evaluation turns observation into improvement.

8
00:01:03.617 --> 00:01:14.543
Teams may evaluate answer quality, groundedness, retrieval accuracy, refusal behavior, workflow completion, user satisfaction, and recurring failure patterns.

9
00:01:14.543 --> 00:01:26.476
Evaluation should be connected to model version, retrieval collection, prompt template, runtime configuration, and deployment environment so teams can understand what changed.

10
00:01:26.476 --> 00:01:30.970
The goal is accountable operation, not uncontrolled surveillance.

11
00:01:30.970 --> 00:01:39.959
Monitoring should help teams detect failures, improve quality, manage cost, and enforce policy without creating a new privacy problem.

12
00:01:39.959 --> 00:01:48.405
A sovereign AI program should make logs and evaluation data useful, protected, and limited to legitimate operational purposes.

13
00:01:48.405 --> 00:01:53.132
It also helps to define who is allowed to review sensitive observations.

14
00:01:53.132 --> 00:01:55.844
Engineers may need operational metrics.

15
00:01:55.844 --> 00:01:59.486
Privacy and security teams may need selected evidence.

16
00:01:59.486 --> 00:02:02.817
Product owners may need aggregate quality trends.

17
00:02:02.817 --> 00:02:06.382
These views should not all expose the same raw content.

18
00:02:06.382 --> 00:02:15.525
Good observability separates signal from exposure, giving each role enough information to act without broadening access unnecessarily.
